## Why This Is Asked
This tests how a junior engineer models read-only environments, data sourcing, and per-env state management without touching prod resources. It mirrors real-world patterns at scale at top tech companies.

## Key Concepts
- Terraform data sources for read-only references
- Per-env backends to segregate state
- Avoiding resource creation in isolated environments

## Code Example
```javascript
data "aws_vpc" "inventory" {
  filter {
    name   = "tag:Environment"
    values = ["inventory"]
  }
}
data "aws_subnet" "inventory" {
  filter {
    name   = "vpc-id"
    values = [data.aws_vpc.inventory.id]
  }
}
```

```javascript
terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket = "tf-state"
    key    = "inventory/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "us-east-1"
  }
}
```

```javascript
output "inventory_vpc_id" { value = data.aws_vpc.inventory.id }
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you verify that inventory plans never propose changes to prod data? 
- How would you automate inclusion of inventory in CI without accidentally applying prod changes?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to structure a tiny Terraform project with proper state backends, a reusable module, and basic CI guards.

## Key Concepts
- Local vs remote backends and state isolation
- Modules and outputs for reusability
- State locking with DynamoDB
- CI gates that validate plan scope

## Code Example
```hcl
# backend for dev (local)
terraform {
  backend "local" {
    path = "terraform.tfstate"
  }
}
```

```hcl
# modules/storage/main.tf
resource "aws_s3_bucket" "bucket" {
  bucket = var.bucket_name
  acl    = "private"
  versioning {
    enabled = var.versioning
  }
}

resource "aws_dynamodb_table" "lock" {
  name           = "tfstate-lock"
  billing_mode   = "PAY_PER_REQUEST"
  hash_key       = "LockID"
  attribute {
    name = "LockID"
    type = "S"
  }
}

output "bucket_name" {
  value = aws_s3_bucket.bucket.bucket
}

output "locks_table_arn" {
  value = aws_dynamodb_table.lock.arn
}
```

```hcl
# modules/storage/variables.tf
variable "bucket_name" { type = string }
variable "versioning" { type = bool; default = true }
```

```hcl
# backend for prod (example)
terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket         = "tfstate-prod"
    key            = "path/to/env/terraform.tfstate"
    region         = "us-east-1"
    dynamodb_table = "tfstate-lock"
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend the module to support versioned backups with lifecycle rules?
- How would you add a simple test to ensure only module-controlled attributes change during plan?
